              "text": "**Yes, the original formula for Coca-Cola did contain cocaine.** \n\nHere is the history of how the drug ended up in the world's most famous soda, how much was actually in it, and how it is still connected to the cocaine industry today.\n\n---\n\n### Why Cocaine Was in Coca-Cola\nCoca-Cola was invented in **1885** by John Pemberton, a pharmacist from Atlanta, Georgia. Pemberton originally created a beverage called \"Pemberton\u2019s French Wine Coca,\" which was a mix of wine, coca leaf extract, and kola nuts. \n\nWhen Atlanta passed local prohibition laws in 1886, Pemberton removed the alcohol and blended the remaining ingredients with carbonated syrup, creating **Coca-Cola**. \n* **\"Coca\"** represented the **coca leaf**, from which cocaine is derived.\n* **\"Cola\"** represented the **kola nut**, which provided caffeine.\n\nIn the late 19th century, cocaine was completely legal, highly unregulated, and viewed as a miracle medicine. It was commonly used in patent medicines, tonics, and throat lozenges to treat fatigue, headaches, and depression. \n\n### How Much Cocaine Was in It?\nThe early formula didn't contain pure, powdered cocaine. Instead, it used a liquid extract made from coca leaves. \n\nBy most historical estimates, an 8-ounce glass of Coca-Cola in the late 1880s contained approximately **9 milligrams of cocaine**. For comparison, a standard recreational dose of cocaine today is significantly higher, but the amount in early Coca-Cola was still enough to give drinkers a distinct physical \"buzz\" and a boost of energy.\n\n### When and Why Was It Removed?\nBy the late 1890s, public and scientific opinion on cocaine began to shift as the public became aware of its highly addictive nature and potential for harm. \n\nAs the stigma grew, Coca-Cola\u2019s head, Asa Candler, began actively trying to reduce the cocaine content. \n* In **1903**, the company officially removed active cocaine from the formula, switching from fresh coca leaves to \"spent\" (decocainized) coca leaves for flavor.\n* However, because early decocainizing technology wasn't perfect, microscopic trace amounts of cocaine may have remained in the beverage until **1929**, when the extraction process was perfected to ensure the drink was entirely drug-free.\n\n### The Modern Connection: How Coke is Made Today\nSurprisingly, **Coca-Cola still uses coca leaves to flavor its soda today.** \n\nTo do this legally, the company relies on a special arrangement with the U.S. government:\n1. A specialty chemical manufacturer in New Jersey called the **Stepan Company** is the only entity in the United States legally authorized by the DEA to import coca leaves (primarily from Peru).\n2. Stepan Company processes about 100 metric tons of dried coca leaves every year. They chemically extract the active cocaine alkaloid from the leaves, leaving behind a cocaine-free flavoring extract. \n3. This decocainized extract is shipped to Coca-Cola to be used in the secret syrup formula.\n4. The extracted cocaine is not destroyed. Instead, Stepan sells it to Mallinckrodt, a pharmaceutical company, which processes it into a prescription local anesthetic used by doctors for delicate ear, nose, and throat surgeries.",
